Writing Kobold-Pascal programs.

Kobold-Pascal is a small, statically typed language in the Pascal family and
the first-class language for in-world programs. It has a familiar, readable
syntax and strong typing, and it has no raw pointers: host objects are reached
through opaque, typed handles, which keeps a script safe to run in the sandbox.
A Kobold program compiles to an RV32 ELF and runs in the VM like any other
guest. See help programs for the program, verb, and agent roles.

Writing one in world:

  1. Author the source in the in-world filesystem with @fedit, in a file whose
     name ends .pas (or .kp), for example /src/hello.pas:

         program hello;
         begin
           WriteLn('Hello from Kobold');
         end.

  2. Compile it to an RV32 ELF with @compile:

         @compile /src/hello.pas

     This writes /bin/hello.elf. @compile runs the Kobold front end, the KPM1
     backend, the assembler, and the linker as sandboxed guests, so it works
     with the security sandbox active. Compiler errors are shown to you.

  3. Bind the image to a program object with @program, then launch it with
     @run, an interactive session where the lines you type are the program's
     input and its output comes back to you. To attach a program as a command
     verb on an object, use @setverb.

The language at a glance:

  Types     integer, real, boolean, char; enum and subranges; set of;
            array[lo..hi] of T and dynamic array of T; record (including
            variant); string; map of T; and opaque host handles.
  Control   if/then/else, while, for..to/downto, repeat/until, case, with,
            break, continue. `and` and `or` short-circuit on booleans and are
            bitwise on integers. The language is case-insensitive.
  Routines  value, var, and const parameters; nested procedures and functions;
            methods attached through a receiver parameter.
  Handles   typed, opaque references to host objects, declared with the
            `interface` syntax; a method call dispatches into the host. A
            script cannot inspect or forge the underlying object.
  Coroutines  `start E` creates a coroutine (a `process` value); resume it by
            calling it; `yield` suspends it. This suits per-tick behaviour.
  Output    WriteLn and its siblings are overloaded routines chosen by argument
            type; a script can add its own overloads.

Full reference:

The complete Kobold-Pascal manuals are kept with the toolchain, under
sdk/toolchain/skjegg/doc/kobold/, for builders working on the server:

  kp-tutorial.md   the language step by step, each step a runnable program
  kp-examples.md   annotated example programs
  kp-language.md   the language reference
  kp-grammar.md    the grammar
  kp-sandbox.md    compiling to a freestanding RV32 ELF for the VM
  kp-guide.md      a one-page overview

Those manuals show the upstream command-line tools rather than the in-world
commands. In boris, use @fedit, @compile, @program, and @run as above.

See also @compile, @program, @setverb, @run, and help programs.
